<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manama is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, on the north-east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain-island">Bahrain Island</a>. Mentioned in Islamic chronicles from the 14th century, it grew as the islands' main port for pearling, trade and later the British political agency. Under the Al <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">Khalifa</a> it became the seat of government, and since independence in 1971 it has developed into a regional banking centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manama.jpg" alt="Manama" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wadiia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manama,_Bahrain_Decembre_2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Manama lies in the north-eastern corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> on a small peninsula along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. The land is flat and arid, with extremely hot summers and mild winters, and reclaimed land has extended the coastline in recent decades. Its coastal position made it a port and trading point of the Gulf. Historically the city was confined to what is now the Manama Souq and the Manama Fort to its south, and it has since grown to take in suburban developments and older neighbouring villages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>There is evidence of human settlement on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>'s northern coastline from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> civilisation inhabited the area in 3000 BCE as a trading hub between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magan">Magan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, and about 100,000 Dilmun burial mounds have been found across the north and centre of the country. The name Manamah is first recorded around 730 AH, about 1330 CE, during a visit by Turan Shah of Hurmuz to the island. Manama remained a small trading port until the Portuguese took control in the early 1500s, and in 1602 the Safavid Persians expelled them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> sidelined Manama and made the nearby town of Bilad Al Qadeem the provincial capital, while the islands' pearl exports grew.</p><p>The twin towns of Manama and Muharraq appear to have been founded together in the 1800s. Muharraq, with its more defensible position, was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> until 1923, while Manama became the mercantile capital and the gateway to the main island. After the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> in 1914 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a> used Manama as a base for the Mesopotamian campaign, and a municipal government was established there in 1919. In the 1920s the British pressed administrative reforms, including a reorganised customs office in 1923, civil courts, and in 1926 the appointment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-belgrave">Charles Belgrave</a> as financial adviser. Manama was declared the capital when Bahrain became independent in 1971.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Manama is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, and its population has long been cosmopolitan: in 1904 it was estimated at 25,000, half of them foreigners from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Hasa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Shia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> have coexisted there for centuries, each represented by distinct ethnic groups. Oil wealth spurred growth in the 20th century, and diversification from the 1990s made the city a financial hub of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and a centre of Islamic banking. Bab Al Bahrain and the adjacent souq are among its notable cultural sites, and the city centre is the main location of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a> processions in the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Bahrain protesters occupied Pearl Roundabout in <b>Manama</b> from February; on 14 March Gulf Cooperation Council forces, mainly Saudi and Emirati troops, arrived at the government’s request, and the camp was cleared.</blockquote><a href="/movement/arab-spring">Arab Spring · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Bahrain lived on pearling, date farming and trade, with <b>Manama</b> as its port.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Sheikhdom of Bahrain</a></li></ul></section>
